 (RPD) is a methodology within the field of Design of Experiments (DOE) that aims to enhance the robustness of products and processes against variability. The primary goal of RPD is to identify optimal settings for design parameters that minimize the effects of noise factors, thereby improving the performance and reliability of products and processes under varying conditions.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**: RPD often employs response surface methodology (RSM) to model the relationship between input factors (design parameters) and output responses. The general form of a response surface model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = f(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k) + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( f \\) is a function of the input factors \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**: RPD typically involves a structured experimental design that may include factorial designs, central composite designs, or Box-Behnken designs. These designs allow for the systematic exploration of the effects of multiple factors on the response variable, facilitating the identification of optimal parameter settings.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The validity of RPD relies on several assumptions, including:\n1. The relationship between factors and responses is approximately linear or can be adequately modeled by a polynomial function.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n3. The variance of the errors is constant across all levels of the factors (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: RPD is particularly applicable in situations where variability in product performance is a concern, such as in manufacturing processes, product design, and quality improvement initiatives. It is selected when the goal is to optimize performance while minimizing sensitivity to external disturbances.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: The analysis in 